{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>You shall not plant for yourself an Asherah.</b> He brought three analogous cases, all of the same theme: things that are pleasing to the senses yet abhorrent because of their spiritual blemish. The first is the Asherah, which is an ornament of palaces, yet was nevertheless abhorrent to the Sanctuary because it served as an appurtenance to idolatry. And correspondingly, Scripture gives precedence to the excellence of the spiritual attribute over the physical perfection of the judge, which is sensory and material. The second is the pillar (מַצֵּבָה): even though it was acceptable before the giving of the Torah, as it says וּשְׁתֵּים עֶשְׂרֵה מַצֵּבָה (Exodus 24:4) — for its significance was as though the one who offers it stands perpetually before the Sanctuary, in the sense of שִׁוִּיתִי ה׳ לְנֶגְדִּי תָמִיד (Psalms 16:8) — they fell from this rank because of the matter of the calf, as it says there: כִּי לֹא אֶעֱלֶה בְּקִרְבְּךָ (Exodus 33:3). And so it is regarding an elder who is not physically attractive, who has acquired a bad reputation in his youth — as opposed to an elder who is physically attractive. Third, he brought the matter of a repugnant blemish: even if the animal is beautiful to the senses and worth a thousand zuz, it is nonetheless disqualified for the Sanctuary because of a blemish that does not even render it missing any blood; whereas an ox worth only a sela is fit for sacrifice because it is whole. And so it is with an elder who possesses a disqualifying trait — as opposed to one who is whole in his character traits, even if he is not wealthy and attractive like the other."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:21:43.056746920Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}